{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_GZB6KrQBBMf1fQpGEFqnstDO","content":"Title: OAuth refresh token errors - API and Webhooks / Authentication - Zoom Developer Forum\nSite: Zoom Developer Forum\nDate: 2024-08-30\nURL: https://devforum.zoom.us/t/oauth-refresh-token-errors/116672\nSnippet: post by app-niuco on Aug 31, 2024 No, they continue using the app. The problem is this invalid token. It was working normally yesterday. post by ojus.zoom on Sep 5, 2024 Can you ask the users to reinstall the app? This could have possibly happened because the latest value of the refresh token wasnt saved.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Never getting a new refresh token on token endpoint - Questions / OAuth/OIDC - Okta Developer Community\nSite: Okta Developer Community\nDate: 2024-12-04\nURL: https://devforum.okta.com/t/never-getting-a-new-refresh-token-on-token-endpoint/31385\nSnippet: post by udrzalv on Dec 4, 2024 I have issues with getting a new refresh token. I always receive the existing refresh token until it just expires. From Okta docs: Refresh access tokens and rotate refresh tokens | Okta Developer > Whether Okta returns a new refresh token with a new access token depends on the refresh token lifetime setting. If the lifetime setting hasn’t expired, when a client makes a request for a new access token, Okta only returns the new access token. After the lifetime settin\n\n---\n\nTitle: OAuth 2.0 - Integrations - Zoom Developer Docs\nSite: zoom.us\nDate: 2019-04-05\nURL: https://developers.zoom.us/docs/integrations/oauth/\nSnippet: Learn how to request, refresh, and revoke OAuth 2.0 access tokens for Zoom APIs across four authorization flows including account, user, device, and client ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: OAuth server token (/oauth/token/) | REST API Solution Guide\nSite: Fortinet Documentation\nURL: https://docs.fortinet.com/document/fortiauthenticator/8.0.0/rest-api-solution-guide/498666/oauth-server-token-oauth-token\nSnippet: OAuth server token (/oauth/token/) Allowed methods |HTTP method|Resource URI|Action| |-|-|-| |POST|/api/v1/oauth/token/|Get token, or refresh token.| To modify query and response formats or filter results using the REST API, see Filtering query results. Response If the `openid` is specified as scope which is the default one at the moment, JWT token is returned as `id_token`. This token consists of three parts: Header, Payload, and keys (separated by dots).\n\n---\n\nTitle: Auth0 /oauth/access_token obtain refresh_token mobile app?\nSite: Stack Overflow\nDate: 2017-04-04\nURL: https://stackoverflow.com/questions/43207261/auth0-oauth-access-token-obtain-refresh-token-mobile-app\nSnippet: I'm using Facebook SDK in my React-Native mobile app and I manage to get a token that I exchange with Auth0 on `/oauth/access_token` to retrieve an `id_token` that I can use as `jwt` to authenticate with my API. The problem: that endpoint doesn't return a `refresh_token` which is vital for a mobile app in order to not ask the user to authenticate every time. How can I obtain an Auth0 `refresh_token` in order to not have the user to login every single day to Facebook and to the entire process aga\n\n---\n\nTitle: OAuth V2/Refresh token Error\nSite: HubSpot Community\nDate: 2019-03-05\nURL: https://community.hubspot.com/t5/APIs-Integrations/OAuth-V2-Refresh-token-Error/m-p/256748\nSnippet: Hi When trying to update the access token, the following response is returned from the API. {\"status\":\"BAD_REFRESH_TOKEN\",\"message\":\"missing or invalid refresh token\",\"correlationId\":\"xxxx\",\"requestId\":\"xxxxx\"} Why does this problem happen? My PHP code ``` $url = \"https://api.hubapi.com/oauth/v1/token\"; $argary = array( 'grant_type' => 'refresh_token', 'client_id' => 'XXXXXXXX', 'client_secret' => 'XXXXXXXX', 'redirect_uri' => 'https://XXXXX/OAuth/auth-callback', 'refresh_token'=> '--My first re\n\n---\n\nTitle: What Are Refresh Tokens and How to Use Them Securely\nSite: Auth0\nDate: 2015-10-07\nURL: https://auth0.com/blog/refresh-tokens-what-are-they-and-when-to-use-them/\nSnippet: Learn more about refresh tokens and how they help developers balance security, privacy, and usability in their applications.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Web API与OAuth：既生access token，何生refresh token\nSite: 博客园\nDate: 2015-07-13\nURL: https://www.cnblogs.com/dudu/p/oauth-refresh-token.html\nSnippet: 注：这段客户端代码与前一步中客户端代码的主要区别是少了下面传递 resource owner 用户名与密码的代码，这就是 refresh token 的用途所在 —— 不需要用户名与密码就可以刷新 access token。 ``` parameters.Add(\"username\", \"[username]\"); parameters.Add(\"password\", \"[password]\"); ``` 运行结果： ``` { \"access_token\": \"[new access token]\", \"token_type\": \"bearer\", \"expires_in\": 86399, \"refresh_token\": \"[new refresh token]\" } ``` 搞定！ 看起来挺简单，却折腾了一天。 希望在你折腾OAuth的时候，这篇博文能够帮你减少折腾的时间。 【参考资料】 Adding Refresh Tokens to a Web API v2 Authorization Server EmbeddedResourceOwn\n\n---\n\nTitle: \"oauth/token/refresh\" always returning unauthenticated\nSite: Laracasts\nURL: https://laracasts.com/discuss/channels/laravel/oauthtokenrefresh-always-returning-unauthenticated\nSnippet: Level 1 Hi, I am trying to use Laravel passport for one of my api based application. I followed the documentation as per the wiki. I am able to login and generate both the access_token and refresh_token using client_id 2. I am able to retrieve the user details using the api api/me. However, whenever I try to refresh the token (even when both the tokens are not expired), I am getting 401 unauthenticated error from AuthenticationException class. Here is my request via postman:\n\n---\n\nTitle: API Documentation - oauth/token - Geoloqi Developers\nSite: geoloqi.com\nURL: https://developers.geoloqi.com/api/oauth/token\nSnippet: Application Token You can use the client_credentials grant to get an access token for an application. Parameters - grant_type client_credentials - client_id Your app's client ID - client_secret Your app's client secret Assertion Applications can get an access token for a user it has created by specifying the user ID. Parameters - grant_type assertion - client_id Your app's client ID - client_secret Your app's client secret - assertion_type user_id (the literal string user_id) - assertion The use\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu OAuth support - Page 3\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-01-30\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759?page=3\nSnippet: Intervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. OAuth allows external applications to request authorization to access an ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu API Integration Cookbook - Guide\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2024-11-20\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-api-integration-cookbook/80090\nSnippet: This tells you everything you need to know to get completed activities into and out of Intervals.icu, to download planned workouts and to ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Request access token via /oauth/token/ endpoint using code - Support / Installation and Deployment - Tyk API Management Community Forum\nSite: Tyk API Management Community Forum\nDate: 2016-01-19\nURL: https://community.tyk.io/t/request-access-token-via-oauth-token-endpoint-using-code/124\nSnippet: links Hi Pascal, I’m afraid not - keys are stored in redis by the key name, there is no real way to search by value at the moment. However, if you use the policies feature to attach a policy to a key, you can bulk control access rights across whole swathes of keys. Regarding the api_id, this parameter does not filter by API, it filters by session handler. Tyk allows you to add custom back-ends to authorization and session handlers (e.g. for LDAP or other DB’s). In order for Tyk to know which ses\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu OAuth support - Announcements\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-01-30\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759\nSnippet: OAuth allows external applications to request authorization to access an Intervals.icu user's data. It allows users to grant and revoke API ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: API access to Intervals.icu - Guide\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2020-04-01\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/api-access-to-intervals-icu/609\nSnippet: Intervals.icu now has a public API for other applications. To use it you need to generate an API key in /settings (look for “Developer ...\n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_LPUVjAt3J6qalOO3XGVYaomv","content":"Title: intervals-icu.md - soma-intelligence/open-health-sync\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-10-12\nURL: https://github.com/soma-intelligence/open-health-sync/blob/main/docs/connectors/intervals-icu.md\nSnippet: Scope Gives ACTIVITY:READ the athlete's activities WELLNESS:READ the athlete's daily wellness records (weight, resting heart rate, HRV, sleep, ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Open-Triathlon-Coach-for-ChatGPT/POLICY_BASELINE.md at main · Takethis88/Open-Triathlon-Coach-for-ChatGPT\nSite: Github\nDate: 2026-07-21\nURL: https://github.com/Takethis88/Open-Triathlon-Coach-for-ChatGPT/blob/main/POLICY_BASELINE.md\nSnippet: ChatGPT plan and Action compatibility Permalink: ChatGPT plan and Action compatibility The GPT itself and its custom Action are treated as separate capabilities. Current project validation as of 23 July 2026: - Plus has passed OAuth, authenticated read and controlled calendar-write tests. - A signed-in Free account could open the GPT, but the Intervals.icu Action was unavailable in that test, so OAuth could not start. - Go has not yet been tested. - Pro mode cannot use custom Actions under OpenA\n\n---\n\nTitle: @kuranov/intervals-client\nSite: npm\nDate: 2026-02-03\nURL: https://www.npmjs.com/package/@kuranov/intervals-client\nSnippet: Examples OAuth2 Flow For public applications serving multiple users, implement the full OAuth2 authorization code flow: ``` // Step 1: Redirect user to authorization URL const authUrl = new URL('https://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize'); authUrl.searchParams.set('client_id', YOUR_CLIENT_ID); authUrl.searchParams.set('redirect_uri', YOUR_REDIRECT_URI); authUrl.searchParams.set('response_type', 'code'); authUrl.searchParams.set('scope', 'ACTIVITY:READ ACTIVITY:WRITE WELLNESS:READ'); authUrl.searchPa\n\n---\n\nTitle: ueberauth_intervals_icu on Hex\nSite: Libraries.io\nDate: 2026-08-13\nURL: https://libraries.io/hex/ueberauth_intervals_icu\nSnippet: Scopes Permalink: Scopes intervals.icu joins scopes with commas, not the spaces used by most OAuth 2.0 providers: ``` providers: [ intervals_icu: {Ueberauth.Strategy.IntervalsIcu, [default_scope: \"ACTIVITY:READ,WELLNESS:WRITE\"]} ] ``` Each scope takes a `:READ` or `:WRITE` suffix: |Scope|Covers| |-|-| |`ACTIVITY`|Activities, intervals, streams| |`WELLNESS`|Wellness records: weight, HRV, resting HR, sleep| |`CALENDAR`|Planned workouts and calendar events| |`CHATS`|Messages| |`LIBRARY`|Workout and\n\n---\n\nTitle: Restream OAuth Scopes\nSite: Accuweather Api\nDate: 2026-07-11\nURL: https://apis.io/scopes/restream/restream-scopes/\nSnippet: 1. Home 2. OAuth Scopes 3. Restream OAuth 2.0derived Restream publishes 7 OAuth 2.0 scopes via the authorizationCode flow. Scopes are the fine-grained permissions an application requests at authorization time to act against the Restream API on a user’s behalf. Tokens are issued from https://api.restream.io/oauth/token. This index is generated from the provider’s OpenAPI security definitions (and, where available, its documented scope reference) and refreshes on every APIs.io network build. Brows\n\n---\n\nTitle: intervals-icu-api | Skills Marketplace\nSite: lobehub.com\nDate: 2026-10-21\nURL: https://lobehub.com/skills/openclaw-skills-intervals-icu-api\nSnippet: Comprehensive guide for interacting with the intervals.icu API to manage athlete training data, activities, workouts, and calendar events.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Fitbit Web API returns 410 Gone: how to keep sleep, HRV and weight flowing into intervals.icu\nSite: DEV Community\nDate: 2026-11-03\nURL: https://dev.to/antivex/fitbit-web-api-returns-410-gone-how-to-keep-sleep-hrv-and-weight-flowing-into-intervalsicu-11la\nSnippet: Route 3. Any phone: read the Google Health API yourself The setup for google-health-to-intervals takes about 20-30 minutes, once: 1. Create your private copy with Use this template. 2. Create a Google Cloud project and enable the Google Health API. 3. On the consent screen: - choose External and leave the publishing status on Testing; - add yourself as a test user; - add three read-only scopes: `googlehealth.sleep.readonly`, `googlehealth.health_metrics_and_measurements.readonly` and `googleheal\n\n---\n\nTitle: Privacy Policy\nSite: BFL Coaching\nURL: https://bflcoaching.com/privacy\nSnippet: 1. Information We Collect 1.3 Information from Other Sources Intervals.icu Integration: When you connect your Intervals.icu account to our platform, we request the following permissions (OAuth scopes): - ACTIVITY:READ - Access to your training activities, including runs, rides, and other workouts - WELLNESS:READ - Access to wellness data such as sleep, stress, and recovery metrics - CALENDAR:WRITE - Ability to add planned workouts to your training calendar - SETTINGS:WRITE - Update your training\n\n---\n\nTitle: Connect Intervals.icu — VéloPeak Wiki\nSite: VéloPeak\nURL: https://velo-peak.com/en/wiki/connect-intervals-icu\nSnippet: When you connect Intervals.icu, VéloPeak requests the following permissions: Read activities — power, heart rate, cadence, Load, Intensity, and training metrics ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Open API\nSite: Intervals.icu\nURL: https://www.intervals.icu/features/open-api/\nSnippet: Upload and download activities, manage wellness data, create workouts, and receive webhooks. Supports OAuth 2.0 and API key authentication.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu API Integration Cookbook - Guide\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2024-11-20\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-api-integration-cookbook/80090\nSnippet: This tells you everything you need to know to get completed activities into and out of Intervals.icu, to download planned workouts and to read ...\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu OAuth support - Announcements\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-01-30\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759\nSnippet: ACTIVITY:READ,WELLNESS:WRITE Requests read access to activities and read and write access to wellness data.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Request for Official MCP Support for AI Tools (ChatGPT, Claude, ...) - Feature Requests - Intervals.icu Forum\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2026-04-01\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/request-for-official-mcp-support-for-ai-tools-chatgpt-claude/126164\nSnippet: post by Eugene_S on 28 Aug Eugene_S's profile This is exactly the gap I built IntervalSync for, so rather than add a “+1” here is what already exists against the spec in the first post. It’s a hosted remote MCP server — nothing to run, no server of your own, no config file. You sign in with Intervals.icu OAuth and paste one URL into Claude, ChatGPT, or Grok. IntervalSync never sees your API key; it holds a revocable token you can withdraw from your workspace, and every connected assistant is lis\n\n---\n\nTitle: Calendly Connector | Lansweeper Platform\nSite: Lansweeper Community\nDate: 2024-09-30\nURL: https://community.lansweeper.com/t5/connectors/calendly-connector/ta-p/85124\nSnippet: Connections OAuth 2.0 Configure the Connection This connection uses OAuth 2.0, a common authentication mechanism for integrations. Read about how OAuth 2.0 works here. |Input|Comments|Default| |-|-|-| |Authorize URL|The OAuth 2.0 Authorization URL for the Calendly API. See Calendly API docs for details.|https://auth.calendly.com/oauth/authorize| |Token URL|The OAuth 2.0 Token URL for the Calendly API. See Calendly API docs for details.|https://auth.calendly.com/oauth/token| |Scopes|Space-separat\n\n---\n\nTitle: Intervals.icu OAuth support - Page 3\nSite: Intervals.icu Forum\nDate: 2021-01-30\nURL: https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759?page=3\nSnippet: Intervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. These are the scopes: ACTIVITY, WELLNESS, CALENDAR, LIBRARY, SETTINGS You need ...\n\nWhen you rely on a result in your answer, cite it inline as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url)."}
{"role":"assistant","content":"The web results are mixed on whether `CALENDAR:*` is a valid Intervals.icu scope — let me read the official Intervals.icu OAuth docs and the connector doc directly.","tool_calls":[{"type":"function","id":"tool_zpixKjZXD34NcaEQVYBiSUqt","function":{"name":"FetchURL","arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://forum.intervals.icu/t/intervals-icu-oauth-support/2759\"}"}},{"type":"function","id":"tool_0UvcdisWORtEzini6eBPwfiu","function":{"name":"FetchURL","arguments":"{\"url\":\"https://github.com/soma-intelligence/open-health-sync/blob/main/docs/connectors/intervals-icu.md\"}"}}]}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_zpixKjZXD34NcaEQVYBiSUqt","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\nIntervals.icu uses OAuth2 for authentication to the API. OAuth allows external applications to request authorization to access an Intervals.icu user’s data. It allows users to grant and revoke API access on a per-application basis and keeps user authentication details safe.\n\n## Heading link Creating An Application\n\nFill in the application form here: https://intervals.icu/oauth/apply\n\nYou need to be logged in as the Intervals.icu athlete that will own the app. This athlete will have access to the management page to edit the app, setup redirect URI’s, webhooks and other things.\n\nYour app will show up on the /settings/apps page (only for you as the owner) and you can click “Manage App” to retrieve your client\\_id and secret, change redirect URL’s, configure webhooks and so on.\n\n\n![image1](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/cb935eaa9a1b9e784347f5a9486fe24a.png)\n\nThe app will have status “Pending” until it is approved. **Please note that you will not be able to do the OAuth flow until it is approved**. You can upload a logo, get the client ID and secret and so on.\n\nOnce it has been approved, the pending tag will disappear. You will receive a system notification when this happens (delivered by email and in the Intervals.icu app).\n\nNote that it will not be visible to other users in /settings/apps, but you can send anyone you like to the OAuth consent page and begin integration.\n\n\n![image2](https://kimi-web-img.kimi.ai/prod-data/online-image/search-upload/ef7703e10049bf07ae1b507e4796aacc.png)\n\nWhen you have completed your integration and tested properly, contact support@intervals.icu to have it listed in the public app directory. Please include a link to the management page in the email.\n\n## Heading link Requesting Authorization To Access Intervals.icu Data For A User\n\nSend the user to:\n\n```lang-auto\nhttps://intervals.icu/oauth/authorize?client_id=<your client id>\n    &redirect_uri=<your redirect uri>\n    &scope=<required scopes>\n    &state=<optional data>\n\n```\n\nIntervals.icu will ask the user to login and display a confirmation dialog with options to choose which\n\nscopes to grant the application. If the user confirms then they are redirected to the redirect\\_uri with an\n\nauthorization code and the optional state parameter:\n\n```lang-auto\n<your redirect uri>?code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4&state=...\n\n```\n\nIf they decline:\n\n```lang-auto\n<your redirect uri>?error=access_denied\n\n```\n\nYour server needs to exchange the code for an access token within 2 minutes by POSTing form data including your client\\_id and client\\_secret:\n\n```lang-auto\ncurl -X POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token \\\n    -d client_id=... \\\n    -d client_secret=... \\\n    -d code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4\n\n```\n\nIf all goes well Intervals.icu will respond with an access token, granted scopes and the id and name of the athlete:\n\n```lang-auto\n{\n    \"token_type\": \"Bearer\",\n    \"access_token\": \"d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9\",\n    \"scope\": \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:WRITE\",\n    \"athlete\": {\n        \"id\": \"2049151\",\n        \"name\": \"David (intervals.icu)\"\n    }\n}\n\n```\n\nTo call the API use “Authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9” header.\n\nNote that you can use “0” for the athlete id for endpoints that accept an athlete id in the path. This will use the athlete for the bearer token used to make the call.\n\n### Heading link PKCE (optional)\n\nPKCE is supported to protect the authorization code from interception. Generate a random `code_verifier` (43-128 chars of `A-Z a-z 0-9 - . _ ~`) and add its challenge to the authorize URL (only `S256` is supported):\n\n```\n    &code_challenge=<BASE64URL(SHA256(code_verifier)) without padding>\n    &code_challenge_method=S256\n\n```\n\nThen include `-d code_verifier=...` when exchanging the code. If a challenge was sent, the verifier is required, and a verifier is rejected if no challenge was sent. The client\\_secret is still required unless your app is a public client.\n\nOnce every version of your app uses PKCE, tick “Require PKCE” on your app’s settings page. Authorizations without a code\\_challenge are then rejected, and so is exchanging a code without a code\\_verifier, even with the client\\_secret.\n\n### Heading link Public clients (SPA, mobile and desktop apps)\n\nApps that cannot keep a client\\_secret confidential can be flagged as public clients on the OAuth app management page. Public clients that use PKCE can omit client\\_secret when exchanging the code. Without PKCE the client\\_secret is still required (this is supported so existing apps can migrate, please switch to PKCE). The automatic http://localhost redirect exception does not apply to public clients, so register the exact redirect URIs you use, including any localhost ones. The port of a registered http://localhost or http://127.0.0.1 redirect is ignored, so native apps can listen on any free port:\n\n```\ncurl -X POST https://intervals.icu/api/oauth/token \\\n    -d client_id=...\n    -d code=3983ed415f66413c890ca48b7cce59e4\n    -d code_verifier=...\n\n```\n\n## Heading link Scopes\n\nScopes are as follows:\n\n- ACTIVITY: Completed rides, runs etc.\n- WELLNESS: Weight, resting HR etc.\n- CALENDAR: Planned workouts\n- CHATS: Chats, groups and messages\n- LIBRARY: Workout library\n- SETTINGS: Athlete settings\n\nFor each scope specify READ or WRITE (to update, implies READ access) and use commas to separate multiple scopes. Example:\n\n```lang-auto\nACTIVITY:READ,WELLNESS:WRITE\n\n```\n\nRequests read access to activities and read and write access to wellness data.\n\n## Heading link Revoking An Access Token\n\nWhen a user disconnects Intervals.icu in your app or deletes their data on your side, revoke the access token:\n\n```lang-auto\ncurl -X DELETE \"https://intervals.icu/api/v1/disconnect-app\" \\\n -H 'authorization: Bearer d842c1fc25f241e5ae440d09756448a9'\n\n```\n\nThis will stop webhook delivery for the athlete.\n\n## Heading link Your Own Data\n\nNote that you don’t need to do all this if you just want access to your own data. Use your API key to do that.\n\n## Heading link API Endpoints"}
{"role":"tool","tool_call_id":"tool_0UvcdisWORtEzini6eBPwfiu","content":"The returned content is the main text extracted from the page. If you use it in your answer, cite this page as a markdown link, e.g. [title](url).\n\n## intervals.icu connector\n\nPermalink: intervals.icu connector\n\nSlug `intervals_icu`. Users connect their own intervals.icu account over OAuth; Open Health\nSync pulls their activities and daily wellness records on a schedule and when intervals.icu\nsends a webhook. You register your own intervals.icu OAuth application: nothing is shared\nbetween instances.\n\nintervals.icu is a training-analysis service that many athletes already feed from Garmin\nConnect, Wahoo, Zwift, Coros, Suunto, Polar and file uploads. One connection here reaches the\ndata of all of those, and each workout says which upstream it came from.\n\nStatus: available. OAuth apps are approved by the intervals.icu developer on request. Tests\nrun against a fake of the intervals.icu API built from the public documentation and forum\nguides.\n\n## Before you start: access and limits\n\nPermalink: Before you start: access and limits\n\n- **Applying for an app.** Sign in to intervals.icu as the athlete who will own the app and\nfill in the short form at intervals.icu/oauth/apply.\nThe app appears under **Settings → Apps** as \"Pending\" and the OAuth flow does not work\nuntil it is approved; you get an email and an in-app notice. Approval time is not published.\nApps listed in the public app directory are a separate request to support@intervals.icu.\n- **Personal API keys are not used.** intervals.icu also offers a personal API key (basic\nauth) for a single athlete. This connector uses OAuth only, so any athlete can connect.\n- **Activities synced from Strava.** intervals.icu does not hand Strava-sourced activities to\nother apps: the API lists them as stubs without figures, and no webhook fires for them. Such\nactivities are skipped. Activities that reach intervals.icu directly (Garmin, Wahoo, Zwift,\nuploads, and so on) are collected.\n- **Garmin data may stop flowing through intervals.icu.** Garmin's developer agreement bars\nlicensees from passing Garmin user data to services Garmin has not approved in writing, and\nthe press reports that Garmin's October 2026 revisions enforce this. Intervals Pro, a coaching\napp built on intervals.icu data, closed on 2026-10-12 over it, and intervals.icu may have to\nhold back Garmin-sourced activities and wellness from its API; no change or date is announced\n( **reported**, the5krunner, 2026-10-14).\nIf that happens, Garmin workouts and wellness stop arriving through this connector while\nother upstreams keep working. For Garmin data, see the Garmin guide.\n- **Rate limits.** intervals.icu does not publish its API rate limits **(to confirm)**. An\nHTTP 429 defers the connection's job until `Retry-After` (one minute when it is missing).\n\n## 1\\. Create the OAuth application\n\nPermalink: 1. Create the OAuth application\n\n1. Apply at intervals.icu/oauth/apply (see above).\n2. Once approved, open **Settings → Apps**, then **Manage App**. Add the redirect URI\n`<OHS_PUBLIC_URL>/oauth/intervals_icu/callback`, for example\n`https://ohs.example.org/oauth/intervals_icu/callback` ( `ohs doctor` prints it).\n3. Copy the **client id** and the **client secret**.\n4. For webhooks (optional, see section 5), set the webhook URL to\n`<OHS_PUBLIC_URL>/inbound/intervals_icu` on the same page, tick the activity and wellness\nevents, and copy the **webhook secret**.\n\n## 2\\. Configure the instance\n\nPermalink: 2. Configure the instance\n\nIn `.env`:\n\n```\nOHS_PUBLIC_URL=https://ohs.example.org\nOHS_INTERVALS_ICU_CLIENT_ID=...\nOHS_INTERVALS_ICU_CLIENT_SECRET=...\n# Webhooks (optional, see section 5):\n# OHS_INTERVALS_ICU_WEBHOOK_SECRET=...\n```\n\nRestart, then run `ohs doctor`:\n\n```\nok    connector intervals_icu\n      intervals_icu callback: https://ohs.example.org/oauth/intervals_icu/callback\n\n```\n\n`CLIENT_ID` and `CLIENT_SECRET` are required: with only one set, `ohs doctor` fails and the\nconnector stays off. `WEBHOOK_SECRET` is optional; without it webhooks are refused (HTTP 401)\nand data still arrives with the scheduled pulls. `GET /v1/providers` lists `intervals_icu`\nwhen it is on.\n\n## 3\\. Scopes\n\nPermalink: 3. Scopes\n\nThe consent page asks for `ACTIVITY:READ,WELLNESS:READ` (intervals.icu separates scopes with\ncommas).\n\n| Scope| Gives|\n| ---| ---|\n| `ACTIVITY:READ`| the athlete's activities|\n| `WELLNESS:READ`| the athlete's daily wellness records (weight, resting heart rate, HRV, sleep, steps, and so on)|\n\nNothing is ever written to intervals.icu.\n\n## 4\\. Connect a user\n\nPermalink: 4. Connect a user\n\nYour server asks for a consent URL (see api.md):\n\n```\ncurl -s -X POST $OHS/v1/users/$USER_ID/connections/intervals_icu \\\n  -H \"Authorization: Bearer $KEY\" -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n  -d '{\"redirect_uri\":\"https://app.example.org/connected\"}'\n```\n\nSend the user to the returned `authorize_url`. After consent they land on your\n`redirect_uri` with `status=connected`. intervals.icu requires the code to be exchanged\nwithin two minutes, which the callback does at once. The token answer names the athlete\n( `athlete.id`), stored as the connection's provider user id so webhooks can be matched to it.\n\nA new connection is backfilled over the last 30 days, then pulled every 6 hours. Requests use\nathlete id `0`, which intervals.icu reads as \"the athlete of this token\".\n\n**Tokens.** intervals.icu issues an access token with no refresh token and no stated expiry;\nconnecting again replaces the previous token. When the API refuses the token (HTTP 401, as\nafter the athlete revokes the app) there is nothing to refresh, so the connection becomes\n`needs_reauth` and the user must connect again. An HTTP 403 is reported as an error but does\nnot mark the connection: it can also mean a missing scope **(to confirm: how intervals.icu**\n**answers a revoked token)**.\n\nDisconnecting the provider calls `DELETE /api/v1/disconnect-app` with the athlete's token,\nbest effort, which revokes the app and stops webhook delivery for that athlete.\n\n## 5\\. Webhooks\n\nPermalink: 5. Webhooks\n\nAn OAuth app has one webhook URL for every athlete who authorized it. intervals.icu POSTs JSON\nof this shape:\n\n```\n{\n  \"secret\": \"<the app's webhook secret>\",\n  \"events\": [\n    {\"athlete_id\": \"i123456\", \"type\": \"ACTIVITY_UPLOADED\", \"timestamp\": \"...\", \"activity\": {\"id\": \"...\", \"start_date\": \"...\"}}\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nThe `secret` is compared, in constant time, with `OHS_INTERVALS_ICU_WEBHOOK_SECRET`; a body\nwithout the right secret is refused with 401. A non-2xx answer makes intervals.icu retry with\nexponential backoff. An event is only a change signal: it queues a pull, read with the\nathlete's own token, for an athlete with an active connection here, so a forged event cannot\ninject data.\n\n| Event| What happens|\n| ---| ---|\n| `ACTIVITY_*` ( `ACTIVITY_UPLOADED`, `ACTIVITY_ANALYZED`, ...)| a pull of activities from one day before to one day after the activity's start; of the last 7 days when the event names no readable start|\n| `WELLNESS_*`| a pull of wellness records around the record's day ( `wellness.id`), or of the last 3 days|\n| anything else ( `CALENDAR_UPDATED`, `SPORT_SETTINGS_UPDATED`, ...)| ignored, answered 200|\n\nSeveral events of one athlete and data type in one POST are merged into one pull. Some events\n( `ACTIVITY_ANALYZED`) arrive about 60 seconds after the change.\n\nWhat is documented and what is not: the `secret` plus `events` envelope, `athlete_id`,\n`type`, `timestamp`, the `activity` object of activity events, and the event names\n`ACTIVITY_UPLOADED`, `ACTIVITY_ANALYZED`, `CALENDAR_UPDATED` and `SPORT_SETTINGS_UPDATED`\ncome from the intervals.icu API cookbook. The wellness event name and its `wellness` object,\nand an activity delete event, are **(to confirm)**; any `WELLNESS_*` event triggers a wellness\npull whatever it carries.\n\n## Data produced\n\nPermalink: Data produced\n\n### Activities → workouts\n\nPermalink: Activities → workouts\n\nEach activity of `GET /api/v1/athlete/0/activities` becomes one workout:\n\n| Workout field| From|\n| ---| ---|\n| `activity_type`| `type`, which uses Strava's names: `Run`, `TrailRun`, `VirtualRun` → `running`; `Ride`, `GravelRide`, `MountainBikeRide`, `VirtualRide` → `cycling`; `Swim`, `OpenWaterSwim` → `swimming`; `WeightTraining` → `traditional_strength_training`; and so on. An unknown type is `other`; the intervals.icu name is always in `metadata.intervals_type`|\n| `start_date`, `end_date`| `start_date` (UTC), or `start_date_local` in the activity's `timezone`; plus `elapsed_time` (s)|\n| `duration_s`| `moving_time` (s), or `elapsed_time` when missing; both are in metadata|\n| `distance_m`, `avg_speed_mps`, `elevation_gain_m`| `distance` (m), `average_speed` (m/s), `total_elevation_gain` (m). A zero distance or speed is left empty|\n| `avg_heart_rate`, `max_heart_rate`| `average_heartrate`, `max_heartrate` (bpm), when recorded|\n| `active_energy_kcal`| `calories` (kcal) when given, otherwise `icu_joules` ÷ 1000, the kilojoules of work shown one to one as kilocalories (the usual convention of power-based tools). `metadata.active_energy_source` says which|\n| `metadata.timezone`| `timezone` (an IANA name)|\n| `metadata.source_provider`| the upstream the activity came from: `source` `GARMIN_CONNECT` → `garmin`, `WAHOO` → `wahoo`, `ZWIFT` → `zwift`, `COROS`, `SUUNTO`, `POLAR`; any other source lowercased ( `upload`, `manual`, ...). The verbatim value is `metadata.intervals_source` **(the full list of `source` values is to confirm)**|\n| `metadata.device_name`| `device_name`, when given|\n| `metadata.indoor`| `trainer`, or a virtual type|\n\nAlso in metadata: `icu_average_watts`, `icu_training_load` and `max_speed` (as\n`average_watts`, `training_load`, `max_speed_mps`). Not collected: the\nactivity name and description (free text), streams (heart rate, power, GPS series), laps,\nintervals and the original files. Workouts are replaced in place when intervals.icu re-sends\nan edited or re-analysed activity (same id).\n\n### Wellness → quantity samples\n\nPermalink: Wellness → quantity samples\n\nEach daily record of `GET /api/v1/athlete/0/wellness` (its `id` is the athlete's local date)\ngives one sample per figure it holds, spanning that date (in UTC: the record carries no time\nzone). All intervals.icu units are metric.\n\n| Wellness field| Quantity| Unit|\n| ---| ---| ---|\n| `weight`| `body_mass`| kg|\n| `restingHR`| `resting_heart_rate`| bpm|\n| `hrv` (rMSSD)| `hrv_rmssd`| ms|\n| `hrvSDNN`| `hrv_sdnn`| ms|\n| `sleepScore`| `sleep_score`| score (0-100)|\n| `steps`| `step_count`| count|\n| `spO2`| `oxygen_saturation`| percent (a value at or below 1 is read as a fraction and stored × 100)|\n| `respiration`| `respiratory_rate`| breaths/min (stored as `bpm`)|\n| `bodyFat`| `body_fat_percentage`| percent (same fraction rule)|\n| `vo2max`| `vo2_max`| mL/kg/min|\n| `systolic`, `diastolic`| `blood_pressure_systolic`, `blood_pressure_diastolic`| mmHg|\n\nA null, missing, zero or negative figure gives no sample. A figure revised later the same day\nreplaces the stored one. Raw only (kept in the raw store and in the samples' metadata, no\ncanonical name yet): `sleepSecs` (sleep duration), `sleepQuality`, `avgSleepingHR` and\n`kcalConsumed`. Not collected: training load figures ( `ctl`, `atl`), mood, soreness, fatigue,\nmenstrual phase and other subjective fields. The wellness record names no upstream device,\nso its samples are attributed to `intervals_icu` itself **(to confirm: whether the API**\n**exposes a per-field source)**.\n\n## Limitations\n\nPermalink: Limitations\n\n- **Sources.** The OAuth flow, the token answer, scopes, athlete id `0` and the webhook\nenvelope follow the intervals.icu forum guides; the field names follow the published API and\nthird-party clients. Something differs from what you see with your account? Open an issue.\n- **Deleted activities** are not applied: no documented webhook names a deletion, and a pull\ncannot tell a deleted activity from one outside the window.\n- **Late uploads.** A scheduled pull asks for activities by start date. An activity uploaded\nmore than a few days after it was recorded is picked up by its webhook (which carries its\nstart) or by a pull whose window covers its start.\n- **Sleep sessions.** intervals.icu gives a nightly duration and score but no bedtime, so no\nsleep session is created."}
