{"role":"assistant","content":"Fixed and verified. There were **three independent causes**, one per symptom; full details are in `ANSWER.md`.\n\n## Causes\n\n**1. Connect button 401'd for everyone.** The SPA rendered Connect as a plain `<a href=\"/api/intervals/auth/init\">`. Clicking it is a document navigation, which can't carry the `Authorization: Bearer` header (the token lives in localStorage and only the fetch wrapper attaches it) — so `require_user()` rejected every athlete. The log confirms the failing request was a navigation with no auth header. Fix: `auth/init` now returns the authorize URL as JSON and the SPA fetches it via `api()` (header included) and then navigates (`server.py` `auth_init`, `static/app.js`).\n\n**2. Authorize step 400 since 2026-10-02.** The calendar-sync release rewrote `SCOPES` as `ACTIVITY:READ,ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE` — both READ **and** WRITE for the same scope. Intervals.icu's docs require each scope specified once (READ or WRITE; WRITE implies READ), and the provider rejects the malformed list before the consent screen. The September grant (`ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ`) shows the shape that used to work. Fix: `SCOPES = \"ACTIVITY:WRITE,WELLNESS:READ,CALENDAR:WRITE\"`, plus the spec-required `response_type=code`.\n\n**3. Wrongful \"expired, reconnect\" after an hour.** Intervals.icu tokens come back with **no `refresh_token` and no `expires_in`** — they don't expire on their own; they only die when the athlete revokes the app (then API calls 401). The server invented a 3600 s TTL and the refresher killed the connection at that deadline — exactly what happened to Rivera ~1 h after onboarding. Fix: missing `expires_in` now means non-expiring (`expires_at=None`), the refresher leaves those connections alone, and only a real 401 from the API marks the connection \"expired, reconnect\".\n\n## Verification\n\nRan the whole flow against a local mock of the documented Intervals.icu API (via the `INTERVALS_BASE` override — no external service was contacted): connect → consent redirect → callback → status `connected`, wellness proxies data, the connection survives past the old one-hour mark, a revoked token (401) flips it to \"expired, reconnect\", and disconnect/reconnect works. The old scope string is rejected by the same provider-side validation; the fixed one is accepted. Mock and scratch files were removed afterward.\n\nOne environment note: port 8766 is currently held by a stale Python process from a different temp workspace (`/private/tmp/h-unWmrw`, PID 84285), so my verification ran on a separate port. I left that process untouched — you may want to clear it before starting this app locally."}
